      "summary": {
        "en": {
          "research_question": "The authors investigate the reasons behind the United Nations Security Council's decision to classify the 2018 Democratic Republic of the Congo Ebola epidemic as a domestic health crisis rather than an international security threat, particularly when compared to the 2014 West African epidemic.",
          "study_design": "This work functions as a peer-reviewed commentary that employs a qualitative approach combining a literature review and document analysis to examine a specific historical case.",
          "population_setting": "The analysis centers on the 2018 Ebola epidemic affecting the North Kivu and Ituri provinces within the Democratic Republic of the Congo.",
          "main_findings": "The authors determine that the failure to elevate the crisis to a global security agenda primarily stemmed from its geographic confinement to the African continent. They note that while framing health emergencies as security matters can mobilize additional funding and personnel, the current approach highlights a persistent pattern of international disregard for regional medical needs.",
          "public_health_relevance": "Understanding the political mechanisms that influence emergency classification helps explain disparities in international aid distribution and response scaling during cross-border epidemics.",
          "limitations": "The evaluation is restricted to a single historical instance and depends entirely on secondary documentary sources rather than primary empirical collection.",
          "gids_interpretation": "This publication provides a conceptual framework for analyzing how geopolitical perceptions shape the classification of infectious disease crises, offering contextual background for understanding historical response patterns rather than generating real-time epidemiological metrics.",
          "provenance": {
